  -> two retargets               -> your character\"),\n p(\"The screenshot below is that chain finishing. On the left, the source footage under review. In the centre, the tracked skeleton drawn in magenta over a MetaHuman named Kellan, who is wearing the motion rather than performing it. On the right, the Performance asset's settings, \", C(\"Body Tracking\"), \" on and \", C(\"Facial Tracking\"), \" off. Bottom right, the solver announcing stage 4 of 5.\"),\n img(\"/projects/metahuman-markerless-macos/performance-window.webp\"),\n p(\"Two things in that image are worth naming, because both cost me a session to understand.\"),\n li(B(\"The character is a preview, not an export.\"), \" Setting \", C(\"Visualization Object\"), \" to a MetaHuman Blueprint makes the toolkit build a retarget chain on the fly, so you judge the capture on a real body instead of a bare skeleton. Nothing is baked yet.\"),\n li(B(\"The magenta skeleton does not move during processing,\"), \" and that is expected rather than broken. The reason is further down, under the still skeleton.\"),\n p(\"Why this matters beyond the novelty: the expensive part of animation is not the software, it is getting a human being to perform in front of the right equipment. A pipeline that accepts any video turns every reference clip you have ever bookmarked into a possible source. That is the whole point, and it is why a stale build identifier was worth an afternoon.\"),\n]\n\n# Le contexte se pose apres le paragraphe de configuration et avant le premier mur, parce qu'on ne\n# peut pas expliquer pourquoi un obstacle merite d'etre franchi avant d'avoir dit ce qu'il garde.\nb[3:3] = neuf\nd[\"blocks\"]=b\nio.open(P,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(json.dumps(d,ensure_ascii=False,indent=1))\nj=json.dumps(d,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint(f\"  blocs : {avant} -> {len(b)}\")\nprint(\"  tirets cadratins :\", j.count(\"—\")+j.count(\"–\"))\nPY\nnpm run build 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Insert the context section and the imag